# The weight p of the choice is a nat program variable, never assigned, and so is q, so the
# bound is asked for every initial p, q >= 0; the choice is reached only where p + q <= 1, where
# p is 0 or 1, a probability, so the guard that makes a run abort at an out-of-range weight never
# fires.  Every run pays tick(1) once, so ert(S, 0) = 1 at every p, q and the lower bound 0.5
# holds.
# Expected: valid.  A run reaching an out-of-range weight is given a value that fails a lower
# bound on an expected runtime; this pins the other direction: where no run reaches one, that
# value must not cost the bound.  The twin of choice_weight_state_above_one_ert_lower_half_unknown.pgcl.
nat p;
nat q;
nat c;
tick(1);
if (p + q <= 1) {
	{
		c := 1;
	}[p]{
		c := 0;
	}
} else {
	c := 0;
}
?Ert[0] >= 0.5
